.TH WC 1 2026-10-20 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
wc \- count lines, words, characters, and bytes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wc
.RB [ \-cmlwL ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wc
counts the lines, words, characters, bytes, and longest line of each
.IR file ,
and prints the counts that the options select, on one output line per
.IR file .
With no
.I file
operands, or for a
.I file
named
.BR \- ,
it reads standard input.
A
.I file
that cannot be opened is reported on standard error and skipped, and the
remaining files are still read.
.PP
The input is read as bytes in a single pass.
It is interpreted as text only as described under
.BR Counts .
.SS Counts
.TP
.RB "Lines (" \-l )
The number of LF bytes.
A last line with no LF is not counted.
.TP
.RB "Words (" \-w )
A word is a run of bytes from 0x21 to 0x7E, the printable ASCII
characters other than space.
A space, tab, LF, VT, FF, or CR, or the end of the input, ends a word.
Any other byte, including NUL, the other control characters, and every
byte from 0x80 up, is ignored: it neither ends a word nor makes one.
A NUL inside a word does not split it, and a run of only non-ASCII
letters is not a word.
.TP
.RB "Characters (" \-m )
In UTF-8 mode, described under
.BR ENVIRONMENT ,
the number of bytes outside the range 0x80 to 0xBF, which can only
continue a UTF-8 character.
The input is not checked for being valid UTF-8, so a stray byte in that
range counts for nothing.
Otherwise every byte is a character, and the count is the byte count.
.TP
.RB "Bytes (" \-c )
The number of bytes read, including CR and every other byte.
.TP
.RB "Longest line (" \-L )
The width in columns of the widest line.
A printable ASCII character, 0x20 to 0x7E, moves the column forward by 1.
A tab moves it to the next multiple of 8.
LF, CR, and FF end the line: its width is compared with the greatest so
far, and the column returns to 0.
VT compares in the same way, but the column stays where it is, so the
text after it continues the line.
Any other byte, including backspace, the other control characters, and
every non-ASCII byte, does not move the column, so accented, wide, and
combining characters are not measured.
A last line with no LF is included.
The CR of a CRLF line ending adds nothing to the width.
.SS Output
Each output line has the selected counts in a fixed order, whatever the
order of the options: lines, words, characters, bytes, and longest line.
With none of
.BR \-cmlwL ,
the counts are lines, words, and bytes.
.PP
Each count is right-aligned in a field of 9 columns, or more for a larger
number, and a space separates the fields.
The counts are followed by a space and the file name exactly as given on
the command line.
With no
.I file
operands the name is left out, and so is the space before it.
For an operand of
.BR \- ,
the name is
.BR \- .
.PP
When exactly one count is selected and there is at most one operand, the
count is printed alone, without padding, so
.B "wc \-l"
on one file prints a number, a space, and the name.
.PP
With more than one operand, a last line gives the totals, with the name
.BR total .
An operand that could not be opened still counts as an operand, so
.B total
is printed even when only one of two files could be opened.
The total for the longest line is the greatest of those of the files, not
their sum.
.PP
Counts are unsigned 64-bit numbers.
An error opening or reading a file is reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
wc: \fIname\fR: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
where
.I name
is the operand as given, or
.B standard input
when there are no operands.
After a read error the counts up to the error are still printed.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-lw ,
and may follow the
.I file
operands, unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
Giving an option more than once is the same as giving it once.
The options only select counts, and their order does not matter.
.B \-\-
ends the options, and a lone
.B \-
is an operand.
Apart from
.BR \-\-help ,
there are no long options.
An unknown option prints the usage message on standard error and exits
with status 1.
.TP
.B \-c
Print the number of bytes.
.TP
.B \-m
Print the number of characters.
In UTF-8 mode this differs from
.BR \-c ;
otherwise it is the same.
.TP
.B \-l
Print the number of lines, which is the number of LF bytes.
.TP
.B \-w
Print the number of words.
.TP
.B \-L
Print the length of the longest line.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded when
.B wc
starts, as needed when it is run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
The first of these that is set decides whether UTF-8 mode is on, which
affects only
.BR \-m .
When its value is exactly
.BR C ,
UTF-8 mode is off.
Otherwise it is on when the ANSI code page is UTF-8, which it is on
Windows 10 version 1903 and later, and off when it is not.
Other values, such as
.BR POSIX ,
do not turn it off.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, the first
.I file
operand ends the options, so that later arguments such as
.B \-l
are file names.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option, a
.I file
could not be opened or read, or standard output could not be written.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe whose reader has exited, found before the
last write; see
.BR NOTES .
.SH EXAMPLES
Count the lines, words, and bytes of a stream:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aqhello world\en\(aq | wc
        1         2        12
.EE
.RE
.PP
Count the lines of several files, with a total:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ wc \-l a.txt b.txt
        2 a.txt
        1 b.txt
        3 total
.EE
.RE
.PP
Capture a bare count in a shell variable:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ n=$(ls *.c | wc \-l)
.EE
.RE
.PP
Find the width of the widest line of a source file, with tabs counted
as in a terminal:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ wc \-L main.c
.EE
.RE
.PP
Count the characters and the bytes of UTF-8 text:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aqh\e303\e251llo\en\(aq | wc \-mc
        6         7
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU coreutils:
.IP \(bu 2
There are no long options such as
.BR \-\-bytes ,
.BR \-\-total ,
and
.BR \-\-files0\-from ,
and no
.BR \-\-version .
.IP \(bu 2
Words and the longest line are counted by ASCII character, whatever the
locale, so a no-break space does not end a word, and a wide character
has no width.
.IP \(bu 2
Counts are padded to a fixed width of 9, not to fit the largest count.
.IP \(bu 2
Every file is read through, even for
.BR \-c ;
its size is not taken from the file system.
.PP
Files and standard input are read as binary, with no CRLF translation,
and output lines end with LF.
A CRLF text file therefore has the right number of lines, but its byte
count includes the CRs.
A CR ends a word, and a Ctrl-Z (0x1A) in a file or pipe is an ordinary
byte, not the end of the file.
When standard input is the console, each line typed ends in CR and LF,
and a Ctrl-Z at the start of a line, then Enter, ends the input.
In UTF-8 mode, non-ASCII text typed at the console is not converted to
UTF-8: it arrives in the console's input code page, such as 437, and
.B \-m
counts it wrongly unless that code page is UTF-8.
.PP
Standard input is not closed after it is read, so a second
.B \-
operand continues where the first stopped.
At the end of a file or pipe it counts nothing, and at the console it
reads until the next Ctrl-Z.
.PP
When standard output is a pipe whose reader has exited, such as
.B head
after the lines it wanted, a write that fails before the end gives
status 141 and no message.
Output still buffered when
.B wc
finishes, which is usually all of it, fails instead with
.B "wc: standard output: Invalid argument"
and status 1.
.PP
A
.I file
may be a Windows path, with a drive letter and either kind of slash, or a
busybox-w32 device name such as
.IR /dev/null ;
see
.BR busybox (1).
It is printed exactly as given.
A directory fails with
.BR "Is a directory" .
On Windows 10 version 1903 or later, file names may contain any Unicode
characters, and on older systems only those of the system code page.
.PP
A pattern containing
.B *
or
.B ?\&
that matches nothing is passed on as written, and fails with
.B Invalid argument
since Windows file names cannot contain those characters.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR dos2unix (1),
.BR grep (1)
